cff-version: 1.2.0
title: CoralMind Knowledge Library
abstract: >-
    A library that traces how a conversation with an AI can help or harm a
    person's mental health, one step at a time: the behaviors visible in a
    transcript, the pathways they combine into, the context that changes those
    pathways, and the impacts they lead to, with the strength of the evidence
    behind each link. Ships as an installable Python package with a loader,
    query, and validation API.
message: >-
    If you use the CoralMind Knowledge Library, please cite it. Cite the release
    you actually used and record its content digest alongside the version --
    kl.version identifies the release, kl.digest identifies exactly which
    entries were in it. Two campaigns citing the same version but different
    digests did not run against the same library, and the digest is the only
    thing that reveals it.
type: dataset
# `dataset` rather than `software`: the citable contribution is the authored
# content, and the package is how it is delivered. Someone citing this is
# almost always citing a harm pathway, not a loader.
authors:
    - name: CoralMind
      website: https://github.com/coralmind | https://coralmind.org
repository-code: https://github.com/coralmind/knowledge-library
version: 0.1.0 # x-release-please-version
# The citable work is the authored catalog. Code is MIT (LICENSE.md).
license: CC-BY-NC-4.0
keywords:
    - mental health
    - AI safety
    - evaluation
    - harm taxonomy
    - ontology
